["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Big animals still fool beginners: a cow moose for a bull in thick willows, a brown black bear for a grizzly, a coyote for a wolf. One wrong call can be a protected animal.</p></aside><ul><li>Each screen shows one animal or one comparison, from several angles.</li><li>Read the caption, then find the mark it names in the photo.</li><li>Bears: colour does not tell you the species. Shape does <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Photos come from western North America unless the caption says otherwise.</li><li>Photos are illustrations. The written field marks are the source of truth.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-bull-side.jpg\" alt=\"Bull, side view. Tall shoulders, hump, long legs, flat antler palms.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bull, side view. Tall shoulders, hump, long legs, flat antler palms. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jacob W. Frank, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alces_alces_1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-bull-front.jpg\" alt=\"Bull, front view, Grand Teton. Antlers spread like two open hands.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bull, front view, Grand Teton. Antlers spread like two open hands. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: GrandTetonNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bull_Moose_Sticking_Tongue_Out_(28257188958).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-young-bull.jpg\" alt=\"Young bull with small antlers, Grand Teton. Count every point.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Young bull with small antlers, Grand Teton. Count every point. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: GrandTetonNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bull_Moose_Portrait_(41410522174).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Biggest deer in the world; black brown, with a long face and a <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Bell\">bell</button> under the throat <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Young bulls carry spikes or small forks; big bulls carry wide palms <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Some seasons are for <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Spike fork bull\">spike fork bulls</button> only: no more than 2 points on one antler (99%, page 3, 2 October 2026 edition).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-cow.jpg\" alt=\"Cow feeding in a lake, Glacier National Park. No antlers.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cow feeding in a lake, Glacier National Park. No antlers. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: naturenps, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cow_moose_(33233257795).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-cow-head.jpg\" alt=\"Cow head. Long drooping nose, big ears, bell under the throat.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cow head. Long drooping nose, big ears, bell under the throat. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Veronika Ronkos, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cow_moose_profile.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-cow-calf.jpg\" alt=\"Cow and calf, Wyoming.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cow and calf, Wyoming. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shiras_Moose_Cow_and_Calf_Seedskadee_NWR_(15072335119).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-calf.jpg\" alt=\"Calf in spring, Alaska, beside its mother.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Calf in spring, Alaska, beside its mother. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWSAlaska, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_calf_(50007803527).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-calf-winter.jpg\" alt=\"Calf in its first winter, Grand Teton. Short face, small body.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Calf in its first winter, Grand Teton. Short face, small body. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: GrandTetonNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_Calf_(48910979441).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>A cow has no antlers. A calf stays with her through its first winter <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>A <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Calf\">calf</button> is a moose in its first year; it is never a spike fork bull (99%, page 3).</li></ul><figure class=\"video\"><video controls preload=\"none\" playsinline src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/f/ff/Moose_calf_walking.webm/Moose_calf_walking.webm.480p.vp9.webm\"></video><figcaption>A moose calf. If you see a calf, the cow is close by. <span class=\"credit\">Video: Alaska Region U.S. Fish &amp; Wildlife Service, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_calf_walking.webm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a>. Needs internet.</span></figcaption></figure>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-elk-bull-side.jpg\" alt=\"Bull near Maligne Canyon, Jasper, Alberta. Dark mane, long swept back antlers.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bull near Maligne Canyon, Jasper, Alberta. Dark mane, long swept back antlers. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Wilson Hui from Calgary, Canada, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bull_Elk_Stag_(32272946993).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-elk-bull-rear.jpg\" alt=\"Bull looking back. Straw coloured rump patch.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bull looking back. Straw coloured rump patch. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS Photo, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_bull_elk_checks_his_surroundings_for_danger._(c16b5aae-0192-4ceb-9ea1-66ec852197ba).JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-elk-cow.jpg\" alt=\"Cow, Glacier National Park. No antlers, dark head and neck.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cow, Glacier National Park. No antlers, dark head and neck. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: GlacierNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cow_Elk_Glacier_National_Park.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-elk-cow-rear.jpg\" alt=\"Cow from behind, Yellowstone. The big pale rump.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cow from behind, Yellowstone. The big pale rump. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cow_elk,_Firehole_River_(32984223473).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-elk-herd.jpg\" alt=\"Cow herd in winter, Yellowstone.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cow herd in winter, Yellowstone. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park from Yellowstone NP, USA, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cow_elk_near_the_North_Entrance_(15905706616).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-elk-bulls-distance.jpg\" alt=\"Bulls at distance in snow, Wyoming.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bulls at distance in snow, Wyoming. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bull_Gathering_(13541386053).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Elk: tan body, dark brown neck and legs, pale rump patch <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Bigger than a deer, smaller and much paler than a moose <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Antlers sweep back over the body with long tines; count the brow tine on elk (99%, page 3).</li></ul><figure class=\"video\"><video controls preload=\"none\" playsinline src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/18/Cervus_canadensis%2C_Yoho_National_Park.ogv/Cervus_canadensis%2C_Yoho_National_Park.ogv.480p.vp9.webm\"></video><figcaption>A bull elk in BC: note the pale rump and dark neck mane. <span class=\"credit\">Video: Hedwig Storch, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cervus_canadensis,_Yoho_National_Park.ogv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a>. Needs internet.</span></figcaption></figure>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bb-side.jpg\" alt=\"Male, near Whistler, BC. No hump; back highest over the rump.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Male, near Whistler, BC. No hump; back highest over the rump. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Charles J. Sharp, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olympic_black_bear_(Ursus_americanus_altifrontalis)_male_Whistler.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bb-face.jpg\" alt=\"Face, Jasper. Straight profile, tall ears.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Face, Jasper. Straight profile, tall ears. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Thomas Fuhrmann, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_black_bear_(Ursus_americanus)_-_Jasper_National_Park_01.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bb-brown.jpg\" alt=\"Brown phase, Yukon. Brown, but still a black bear.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Brown phase, Yukon. Brown, but still a black bear. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oso_negro_(Ursus_americanus),_Parque_natural_provincial_Tatshenshini-Alsek,_Yuk%C3%B3n,_Canad%C3%A1,_2017-08-25,_DD_84.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bb-cinnamon.jpg\" alt=\"Cinnamon phase, Waterton, Alberta.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cinnamon phase, Waterton, Alberta. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Traveler100, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blackbearwaterton.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bb-blonde.jpg\" alt=\"Pale brown black bear, Dempster Highway, Yukon.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Pale brown black bear, Dempster Highway, Yukon. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Naturedata, CC0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Bear_on_the_Dempster_Highway.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bb-tofino.jpg\" alt=\"On the beach at low tide, Tofino, Vancouver Island.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>On the beach at low tide, Tofino, Vancouver Island. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Charles J. Sharp, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vancouver_Island_black_bear_(Ursus_americanus_vancouveri)_Tofino_4.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Black bears come in black, brown, cinnamon and blonde <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Colour phase\">colour phases</button> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>No open season on the white (Kermode) or blue (Glacier) phases (98%, page 80, Notice to Black Bear Hunters).</li></ul><figure class=\"video\"><video controls preload=\"none\" playsinline src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/25/YohoNationalpark-Ursus_americanus-Amerikanischer_Schwarzb%C3%A4r.ogv/YohoNationalpark-Ursus_americanus-Amerikanischer_Schwarzb%C3%A4r.ogv.480p.vp9.webm\"></video><figcaption>A black bear in BC. Colour alone does not tell black from grizzly. <span class=\"credit\">Video: Hedwig Storch, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YohoNationalpark-Ursus_americanus-Amerikanischer_Schwarzb%C3%A4r.ogv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a>. Needs internet.</span></figcaption></figure>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bb-sow-cubs.jpg\" alt=\"Black bear sow with a black cub and a cinnamon cub, Yellowstone.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Black bear sow with a black cub and a cinnamon cub, Yellowstone. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Neal Herbert, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_bear_with_cubs_(772a9ae1-a841-4d01-910a-484d8842cda0).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-griz-sow.jpg\" alt=\"Grizzly sow with a yearling cub, Yellowstone.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Grizzly sow with a yearling cub, Yellowstone. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS/Jim Peaco, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grizzly_sow_and_yearling_(f3cf0ba1-1dd8-b71b-0b5a-6f044194d3ea).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</div></aside>","<ul><li>Unlawful: \"To hunt a black bear less than 2 years old or any bear in its company\" (98%, page 11, item 14).</li></ul><ul><li>Plain English: no cubs, no yearlings, and not the sow with them <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">98%</span>. Watch any bear for several minutes before you decide (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-griz-front.jpg\" alt=\"Grizzly in sagebrush, Yellowstone. Hump, dished face, short round ears.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Grizzly in sagebrush, Yellowstone. Hump, dished face, short round ears. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS/Jim Peaco, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:File-Grizzly_bear_on_Swan_Lake_Flats;-Jim_Peaco;-June_2005;-Catalog_18220d;-Original_IT8M3358_(f3aced99-1dd8-b71b-0bfc-4ad5c6105cb2).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-griz-side.jpg\" alt=\"Walking in snow. The hump is the highest point of the back.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Walking in snow. The hump is the highest point of the back. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS/Jim Peaco, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grizzly_bear_walking_on_top_of_snow_near_Obsidian_Creek._(101778c8-84ea-4999-8bcc-798e123ce44f).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-griz-profile.jpg\" alt=\"On a gravel bar, Alaska. The hump shows at distance.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>On a gravel bar, Alaska. The hump shows at distance. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS Photo, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grizzly_on_the_Noatak_River_(3925fb42-1dd8-b71c-0799-6752404c98d4).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-griz-face.jpg\" alt=\"Face. Dish between the eyes and nose, small round ears.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Face. Dish between the eyes and nose, small round ears. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Terry Tollefsbol, NATIONAL CONSERVATION TRAINING CENTER-PUBLICATIONS AND TRAINING MATERIALS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grizzly_Bear_Yellowstone.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>No open season on grizzly bears anywhere in BC (98%, page 19).</li><li>Marks: <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Shoulder hump\">shoulder hump</button>, <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Dished face\">dished face</button>, short round ears, long pale front claws <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bb-side.jpg\" alt=\"Black bear: no hump, rump highest, straight face.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Black bear: no hump, rump highest, straight face. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Charles J. Sharp, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olympic_black_bear_(Ursus_americanus_altifrontalis)_male_Whistler.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-griz-side.jpg\" alt=\"Grizzly: hump highest, rump lower.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Grizzly: hump highest, rump lower. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS/Jim Peaco, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grizzly_bear_walking_on_top_of_snow_near_Obsidian_Creek._(101778c8-84ea-4999-8bcc-798e123ce44f).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bb-face.jpg\" alt=\"Black bear: tall ears, straight nose line.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Black bear: tall ears, straight nose line. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Thomas Fuhrmann, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_black_bear_(Ursus_americanus)_-_Jasper_National_Park_01.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-griz-face.jpg\" alt=\"Grizzly: short round ears, dished face.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Grizzly: short round ears, dished face. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Terry Tollefsbol, NATIONAL CONSERVATION TRAINING CENTER-PUBLICATIONS AND TRAINING MATERIALS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grizzly_Bear_Yellowstone.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Clue</th><th>Black bear</th><th>Grizzly</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shoulder</td><td>No hump <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Big muscle hump <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Face</td><td>Straight from forehead to nose <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Dished <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Ears</td><td>Tall, pointed <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Short, round <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Front claws</td><td>Short, dark, curved <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Long, pale, straighter <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Colour</td><td>Any colour <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Any colour, often silver tips <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bighorn-ram.jpg\" alt=\"Ram, Grand Teton. Massive brown horns curling back, down and forward.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Ram, Grand Teton. Massive brown horns curling back, down and forward. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: GrandTetonNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bighorn_Ram_(28284978928).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/bighorn-ram-full-curl.jpg\" alt=\"Mature ram in winter. Horns near a full curl.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Mature ram in winter. Horns near a full curl. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: YellowstoneNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bighorn_sheep_ram_in_winter_(53481643872).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bighorn-pair.jpg\" alt=\"Ram (left) and ewe, Wyoming. Compare the horns.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Ram (left) and ewe, Wyoming. Compare the horns. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Bureau of Land Management, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:My_Public_Lands_Roadtrip-_Wildlife_in_Wyoming_(19912484675).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/bighorn-ewe.jpg\" alt=\"Ewe. Short, thin, slightly curved horns.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Ewe. Short, thin, slightly curved horns. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bighorn_Sheep_Ewe_at_NER_(22730007468).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Bighorn: brown body, white muzzle, white belly and rump patch <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Ram horns are heavy and curl; ewe horns are short and thin, like a goat's <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/mountain-goat-billy.jpg\" alt=\"White shaggy coat, short black horns, beard.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>White shaggy coat, short black horns, beard. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Forest Service Alaska Region, USDA, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_Goat-Tongass-BLL-018_(52502328982).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-goat-face.jpg\" alt=\"Glacier National Park. Long white face, black dagger horns.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Glacier National Park. Long white face, black dagger horns. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: GlacierNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_Goat_-_Oreamnos_americanus_(51361810348).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-goat-radium.jpg\" alt=\"Near Radium, BC, shedding its winter coat in summer.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Near Radium, BC, shedding its winter coat in summer. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Charles J. Sharp, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_goat_(Oreamnos_americanus)_Radium.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/mountain-goat-nanny-kid.jpg\" alt=\"Nanny with her kid on a cliff top.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Nanny with her kid on a cliff top. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park, USA by Diane Renkin, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_goats_on_Sepulcher_Mountain_(8468633942).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Mountain goat: all white, black dagger horns on both sexes, black hooves <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>A sheep's horns are amber or brown, never black <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/cougar.jpg\" alt=\"Cougar. Plain tan coat, long heavy tail with a black tip.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cougar. Plain tan coat, long heavy tail with a black tip. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: National Park Service, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_Lion_in_Grand_Teton_National_Park.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/cougar-kitten.jpg\" alt=\"Cougar kitten. Spots mean a kitten.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cougar kitten. Spots mean a kitten. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Santa Monica Mountains National Recreation Area, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_Lion_Kittens_P-70-73_(44472721211).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/canada-lynx.jpg\" alt=\"Canada lynx, Yukon. Long ear tufts, huge paws, short black tipped tail.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Canada lynx, Yukon. Long ear tufts, huge paws, short black tipped tail. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: kdee64 (Keith Williams), CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canadian_lynx_by_Keith_Williams.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/bobcat.jpg\" alt=\"Bobcat. Spotted, barred legs, tail black on top only.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bobcat. Spotted, barred legs, tail black on top only. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Joyce Cory on Flickr as docentjoyce, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bobcat_photo.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-cougar-glacier.jpg\" alt=\"Cougar, Glacier National Park. Small round head, plain tawny coat, heavy legs.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cougar, Glacier National Park. Small round head, plain tawny coat, heavy legs. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: National Park Service, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_Lion_in_Glacier_National_Park.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-lynx.jpg\" alt=\"Canada lynx in summer. Ear tufts, long legs, big paws.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Canada lynx in summer. Ear tufts, long legs, big paws. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: timboucher, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lynx_canadensis_390072761.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Cougar tail is long, about a third of its body <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Lynx and bobcat tails are short <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Lynx tail tip is black all around; bobcat tail is black on top, white under <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/grey-wolf.jpg\" alt=\"Grey wolf, Yellowstone. Long legs, big feet, broad muzzle.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Grey wolf, Yellowstone. Long legs, big feet, broad muzzle. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park from Yellowstone NP, USA, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_and_grey_wolf_(female_from_Druid_pack,%22Half_Black%22)_walking_in_road_near_Lamar_River_bridge_(16055830467).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/coyote.jpg\" alt=\"Coyote, Yosemite. Slim, pointed muzzle, tall ears.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Coyote, Yosemite. Slim, pointed muzzle, tall ears. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yathin S Krishnappa, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canis_latrans_(Yosemite,_2009).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wolf-grey.jpg\" alt=\"Grey wolf in snow, Yellowstone. Long legs, broad head.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Grey wolf in snow, Yellowstone. Long legs, broad head. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park from Yellowstone NP, USA, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_and_grey_wolf_(female_from_Druid_pack,-Half_Black-)_walking_in_road_near_Lamar_River_bridge_(cropped).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-coyote-snow.jpg\" alt=\"Coyote in snow, Yosemite. Slim, pointed muzzle, tall ears.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Coyote in snow, Yosemite. Slim, pointed muzzle, tall ears. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yathin S Krishnappa, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canis_latrans_(Yosemite,_2009).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wolf-black.jpg\" alt=\"Dark wolf, Yellowstone. Wolves can be black.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Dark wolf, Yellowstone. Wolves can be black. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wolf_at_Biscuit_Basin_(50087877481).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-coyote-howl.jpg\" alt=\"Coyote, Colorado. Rusty ears and legs, bushy tail.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Coyote, Colorado. Rusty ears and legs, bushy tail. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Howl_(cropped).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Wolf: 30 to 50 kg in BC; coyote: 9 to 23 kg <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Wolf: broad blocky muzzle, short rounded ears. Coyote: narrow pointed muzzle, tall pointed ears <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Coyotes run with the tail down; wolves carry it straight out (Tip).</li></ul><figure class=\"video\"><video controls preload=\"none\" playsinline src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/a/ae/Saerbeck_-_Wildfreigehege_N%C3%B6ttler_Berg_-_Canis_lupus_occidentalis_01_ies.webm/Saerbeck_-_Wildfreigehege_N%C3%B6ttler_Berg_-_Canis_lupus_occidentalis_01_ies.webm.480p.vp9.webm\"></video><figcaption>Wolves: big feet, long legs, straight tail. Bigger than a coyote. <span class=\"credit\">Video: Frank Vincentz, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saerbeck_-_Wildfreigehege_N%C3%B6ttler_Berg_-_Canis_lupus_occidentalis_01_ies.webm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a>. Needs internet.</span></figcaption></figure>","<aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Bears: look for the hump and the face, never the colour. If you are not sure, it is a grizzly, and you do not shoot.</p></aside>","<aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Calling a brown or cinnamon bear a grizzly, or a black grizzly a black bear. Check hump, face and ears.</li><li>Shooting a cow moose in willows because only the body showed. Wait to see the head.</li><li>Shooting a lone bear without waiting for cubs to show up behind it.</li></ul></aside>","<aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><ul><li>Every bear you see: say \"hump or no hump, dish or straight, ears tall or round\" before anything else.</li><li>Every moose: find the head and count points on one antler.</li><li>Every canid: weigh it in your head. Mallard sized head and slim legs, coyote. Big blocky head, wolf.</li></ul></aside>","<ul><li>BC <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, 2 October 2026 edition</a>, read directly: moose, calf and elk definitions page 3; unlawful acts page 11, item 14; grizzly reminder page 19; Notice to Black Bear Hunters page 80. Checked 2026-10-02 (98 to 99%).</li><li>Field marks follow the Moose, Elk, Black bear, Mountain sheep, Mountain goat and Predators sessions in this app <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Photos: Wikimedia Commons, public domain or Creative Commons. Credits under each photo.</li></ul>"]